    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nNicholas Branigan's paper is written as a \"student justice decision\" in response to the actual case of Lee v Tam, which appears on the U.S. Supreme Court's 2016-17 docket. Writing within the format of this real-world judicial genre, Branigan argues that the Supreme Court reaffirm its holding that corporate expression -- though distinct from private speech, which is protected -- contributes meaningfully to public debate and deserves proportionate protection.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
